Because reducers are pure, undo does not belong inside them. For each action you support, emit a paired *inverse* action that the app dispatches to roll the change back:

```ts
function invert(op: Operation): Action | null {
  switch (op.type) {
    case "ADD_TODO":      return removeTodo({ id: op.input.id });
    case "REMOVE_TODO":   return addTodo({ id: op.input.id, title: op.input.restoreTitle });
    case "COMPLETE_TODO": return uncompleteTodo({ id: op.input.id });
    default:              return null;
  }
}
```

Dispatching the inverse produces a *new* operation in the log. You keep the history, the effect is undone, and nothing about the log is rewritten. Note what this forces on your schema: `REMOVE_TODO` can only be inverted if its input carries enough to reconstruct the item (hence `restoreTitle`). Designing for invertibility is a schema decision made when you specify the operation, not a feature you bolt on later.

**Do not read this as "the framework has no undo."** It does: `UNDO` and `REDO` are base document actions with input `{ count: Int }` (`packages/shared/document-model/types.ts`), implemented by `undoOperationV2` / `processUndoRedo` in the reducer core. That mechanism works by incrementing the index and marking the undone tail with `skip`, so consecutive NOOPs are collapsed during rebuild and garbage collection.

The two are different tools. Base `UNDO` is a generic "take back the last N operations" over the raw log. The inverse-action pattern is domain-level: it expresses the reversal as a first-class business event that peers, processors, and the audit trail all see as an intentional change. Reach for the inverse when the reversal is meaningful to the domain.

_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/02-Learn/08-deep-dives/01-reducers-in-depth.mdx; base UNDO/REDO confirmed in packages/shared/document-model/{types,operations,reducer}.ts (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._
